/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*-----------------------*\
  Component (CASE-LOCAL): boron, LUMPED -- the total of boric acid
  B(OH)3 and borate B(OH)4-, expressed AS B (the basis the Toray case study
  states: "Boron (B) 5.3 mg/L" in its Table 2, "boron rejection 91 % at pH 8"
  and "95 % at pH 10" in its Table 1).

  WHY ONE LUMPED SOLUTE AND NOT TWO SPECIES.  The membrane module of this
  engine moves each declared solute by its OWN rejection and does not couple
  the feed's speciation (the pH) to the transport: a neutral boric acid and
  an anionic borate cannot be given different permeances by pH, and nothing
  in the flowsheet changes a stream's pH.  So boron is ONE solute whose
  observed rejection is the manufacturer's figure at the pH of each pass
  (the case's module records carry both tests).  The two species are in
  data/standards/species (H3BO3, BOH4) but no boric-acid/borate
  equilibrium record exists in data/standards/chemistry, and none is invented
  here.  See the case's flowsheetDict header for the approximation and for
  the physics a follow-up must add.

  Only the facts the engine reads are stated: the molar mass of the element
  (10.81 g/mol, the standard atomic weight of boron) and that it is a
  nonvolatile non-electrolyte solute at the dilution of seawater (5.3 mg/L).
  No thermochemistry is declared: the case is not a first-law case (its
  component set has no liquid heat capacity for NaCl, as membrane27's).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

name           boron;
formula        B;
MW             10.81;          // g/mol, standard atomic weight of boron
role           nonvolatile;
Vliq           3.9e-5;         // m3/mol -- ASSUMED, UNVERIFIED: the order of magnitude of the partial molar volume of aqueous boric acid (~39 cm3/mol, recalled, not read from a source); the pump model requires a liquid molar volume, and at 0.49 mmol/L its effect on the stream's volume is 2e-5 of it -- to be replaced by a cited value
//  liquidHeatCapacity -- a PLACEHOLDER the pump's enthalpy leg requires of a nonvolatile
//  solute (the engine refuses the ideal-gas fallback).  ASSUMED, UNVERIFIED, NO value is
//  claimed for aqueous boron: 100 J/(mol K) is the order of a small aqueous molecule, and
//  at 5.3 mg/L the solute is 9e-6 of the stream's moles, so it moves no number the case
//  reports; the plant's first law is UNAVAILABLE here in any case (NaCl has no liquid Cp).
liquidHeatCapacity
{
    model         polynomial;
    coefficients  (100.0);
    Trange        (273.15  323.15);
}
dissociation   1;          // one particle in the van't Hoff osmotic pressure (5.3 mg/L: negligible beside the salt)
